# UI Rules

Any future UI should make the research workflow clearer without turning the project into an investment advice product.

## Product Positioning

- The first screen should communicate research status, evidence quality, and data freshness.
- Do not present any view as a trading cockpit, broker terminal, or execution dashboard.
- Do not add trade buttons, order tickets, broker account controls, or buy/sell callouts.

## Research Display

- Every factor view should show hypothesis, input data, validation window, IC/rank IC, grouped returns, walk-forward result, and known caveats.
- Show source and timestamp for every data-backed metric.
- Clearly label simulated research results as research simulations.
- Separate hypothesis, statistical evidence, interpretation, and risk warnings.
- Prefer neutral labels such as "Candidate", "Watch", "Needs More Data", "Statistically Supported", and "Rejected".

## Visual Design

- Use compact, scan-friendly layouts for research tables and dashboards.
- Use color carefully. Avoid green/red language that implies trade action.
- Make warnings visible when data is stale, missing, sparse, or statistically weak.
- Keep charts reproducible by showing the date range, horizon, universe, and grouping method.

## Compliance Guardrails

- Do not display personalized investment advice.
- Do not display deterministic claims about future returns.
- Do not hide uncertainty or sample-size limitations.

